{"abstract":"We generalize the classical nuts and bolts problem to a setting where the input is a collection of n nuts and m bolts, and there is no promise of any matching pairs. It is not allowed to compare a nut directly with a nut or a bolt directly with a bolt, and the goal is to perform the fewest nut-bolt comparisons to discover the partial order between the nuts and bolts. We term this problem bipartite sorting. We show that instances of bipartite sorting of the same size exhibit a wide range of complexity, and propose to perform a fine-grained analysis for this problem. We rule out straightforward notions of instance-optimality as being too stringent, and adopt a neighborhood-based definition. Our definition may be of independent interest as a unifying lens for instance-optimal algorithms for other static problems existing in literature. This includes problems like sorting (Estivill-Castro and Woods, ACM Comput. Surv. 1992), convex hull (Afshani, Barbay and Chan, JACM 2017), adaptive joins (Demaine, López-Ortiz and Munro, SODA 2000), and the recent concept of universal optimality for graphs (Haeupler, Hladík, Rozhoň, Tarjan and Tětek, 2023). As our main result on bipartite sorting, we give a randomized algorithm that is within a factor of O(log³(n+m)) of being instance-optimal w.h.p., with respect to the neighborhood-based definition. As our second contribution, we generalize bipartite sorting to DAG sorting, when the underlying DAG is not necessarily bipartite. As an unexpected consequence of a simple algorithm for DAG sorting, we rule out a potential lower bound on the widely-studied problem of sorting with priced information, posed by (Charikar, Fagin, Guruswami, Kleinberg, Raghavan and Sahai, STOC 2000). In this problem, comparing keys i and j has a known cost c_{ij} ∈ ℝ^+ ∪ {∞}, and the goal is to sort the keys in an instance-optimal way, by keeping the total cost of an algorithm as close as possible to ∑_{i=1}^{n-1} c_{x(i)x(i+1)}. Here x(1) < ⋯ < x(n) is the sorted order. While several special cases of cost functions have received a lot of attention in the community, no progress on the general version with arbitrary costs has been reported so far. One reason for this lack of progress seems to be a widely-cited Ω(n) lower bound on the competitive ratio for finding the maximum. This Ω(n) lower bound by (Gupta and Kumar, FOCS 2000) uses costs in {0,1,n, ∞}, and although not extended to sorting, this barrier seems to have stalled any progress on the general cost case. We rule out such a potential lower bound by showing the existence of an algorithm with a Õ(n^{3/4}) competitive ratio for the {0,1,n,∞} cost version. This generalizes the setting of generalized sorting proposed by (Huang, Kannan and Khanna, FOCS 2011), where the costs are either 1 or infinity, and the cost of the cheapest proof is always n-1.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Jacob, Riko","Goswami, Mayank"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-759389"],"filters":{"added":"2024-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","City University of New York"],"keyword":["Instance Optimality","Nuts and Bolts","Priced Information","Sorting"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on instance optimal algorithms for a generalization of nuts and bolts and generalized sorting","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.APPROX/RANDOM.2024.23","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-759389","identifier":[{"id":"85204422353","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-1288d220-caca-4d38-876f-54963e44adb9"],"keyword":["Instance Optimality","Nuts and Bolts","Priced Information","Sorting"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-860F016154B53493221C9C0BD19430B9"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.APPROX/RANDOM.2024.23","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"city university of new york","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"City University of New York","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Riko","key":"jacobpau","last":"Jacob","locid":"e6f70fb0-caf1-4736-b583-b0777bc1390f","name":"Jacob, Riko","orcid":"0000-0001-9470-1809","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mayank","key":"goswamipau","last":"Goswami","name":"Goswami, Mayank","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4230/lipics.approx/random.2024.23","issn":["18688969"],"journal":"Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics","pages":"1-23","publisher":"Schloss Dagstuhl - Leibniz-Zentrum fuer Informatik GmbH","status":"p","title":"Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics","title_org":"Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics (LIPIcs)","volume":"317"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85204422353"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-1288d220-caca-4d38-876f-54963e44adb9"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"On Instance-Optimal Algorithms for a Generalization of Nuts and Bolts and Generalized Sorting","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-759389","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"The problem of sorting with priced information was introduced by [Charikar, Fagin, Guruswami, Kleinberg, Raghavan, Sahai (CFGKRS), STOC 2000]. In this setting, different comparisons have different (potentially infinite) costs. The goal is to find a sorting algorithm with small competitive ratio, defined as the (worst-case) ratio of the algorithm’s cost to the cost of the cheapest proof of the sorted order. The simple case of bichromatic sorting posed by [CFGKRS] remains open: We are given two sets A and B of total size N, and the cost of an A-A comparison or a B-B comparison is higher than an A-B comparison. The goal is to sort A ∪ B. An Ω(log N) lower bound on competitive ratio follows from unit-cost sorting. Note that this is a generalization of the famous nuts and bolts problem, where A-A and B-B comparisons have infinite cost, and elements of A and B are guaranteed to alternate in the final sorted order. In this paper we give a randomized algorithm InversionSort with an almost-optimal w.h.p. competitive ratio of O(log³ N). This is the first algorithm for bichromatic sorting with a o(N) competitive ratio.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Jacob, Riko","Goswami, Mayank"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-759401"],"event":{"acronym":"ITCS","title":"Innovations in Theoretical Computer Science Conference"},"filters":{"added":"2024-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","City University of New York"],"keyword":["Nuts and Bolts","Priced Information","Sorting"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"an algorithm for bichromatic sorting with polylog competitive ratio","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ITCS.2024.56","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Goswami, Mayank: Supported by NSF grant CCF-1910873"],"id":"S01-000.00-759401","identifier":[{"id":"85184141571","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-5b5d49bc-0b97-46ba-9343-88a246d1c281"],"keyword":["Nuts and Bolts","Priced Information","Sorting"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-71D4EB385CA4864018F415111029ECE4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ITCS.2024.56","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"city university of new york","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"City University of New York","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Riko","key":"jacobpau","last":"Jacob","locid":"e6f70fb0-caf1-4736-b583-b0777bc1390f","name":"Jacob, Riko","orcid":"0000-0001-9470-1809","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mayank","key":"goswamipau","last":"Goswami","name":"Goswami, Mayank","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4230/lipics.itcs.2024.56","issn":["18688969"],"journal":"Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics","pages":"1-17","publisher":"Schloss Dagstuhl - Leibniz-Zentrum fuer Informatik GmbH","status":"p","title":"Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics","title_org":"Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics (LIPIcs)","volume":"287"},"review":"No Review","scoID":["2-s2.0-85184141571"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-5b5d49bc-0b97-46ba-9343-88a246d1c281"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"An Algorithm for Bichromatic Sorting with Polylog Competitive Ratio","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-759401","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"A saddlepoint of an n × n matrix is an entry that is the maximum of its row and the minimum of its column. Saddlepoints give the value of a two-player zero-sum game, corresponding to its pure-strategy Nash equilibria; efficiently finding a saddlepoint is thus a natural and fundamental algorithmic task. For finding a strict saddlepoint (an entry that is the strict maximum of its row and the strict minimum of its column) we recently gave an O(n log∗ n)-time algorithm, improving the O(n log n) bounds from 1991 of Bienstock, Chung, Fredman, Schäffer, Shor, Suri and of Byrne and Vaserstein. In this paper we present an optimal O(n)-time algorithm for finding a strict saddlepoint based on random sampling. Our algorithm, like earlier approaches, accesses matrix entries only via unit-cost binary comparisons. For finding a (non-strict) saddlepoint, we extend an existing lower bound to randomized algorithms, showing that the trivial O(n2) runtime cannot be improved even with the use of randomness.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Dallant, Justin","Haagensen, Frederik","Jacob, Riko","Kozma, László","Wild, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-818237"],"event":{"acronym":"ESA","title":"European Symposium on Algorithms"},"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Belgium","Germany","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Free University of Berlin","University Libre du Bruxelles","University of Liverpool"],"keyword":["Nash equilibria","Randomized algorithm","Time complexity","saddlepoint"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"an optimal randomized algorithm for finding the saddlepoint","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPICS.ESA.2024.44","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-818237","identifier":[{"id":"85205695757","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-4448e628-9e81-4a2d-8f2e-7f20d7123fc1"],"keyword":["matrix-game","nash-equilibria","randomized-algorithm","saddlepoint","time-complexity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-932F2EA775045A3862A95AF7DAAF5C4E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPICS.ESA.2024.44","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.06512","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"be","key":"university libre du bruxelles","loc":["Belgium","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University Libre du Bruxelles","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"free university of berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Free University of Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"gb","key":"university of liverpool","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Liverpool","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Justin","key":"dallantpau","last":"Dallant","name":"Dallant, Justin","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Frederik","key":"haagensenpau","last":"Haagensen","locid":"672280b6-17c9-4191-9b00-8f1322fc1d90","name":"Haagensen, Frederik","orcid":"0000-0002-4161-4442","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Riko","key":"jacobpau","last":"Jacob","locid":"e6f70fb0-caf1-4736-b583-b0777bc1390f","name":"Jacob, Riko","orcid":"0000-0001-9470-1809","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"László","key":"kozmapau","last":"Kozma","name":"Kozma, László","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"wildpau","last":"Wild","name":"Wild, Sebastian","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4230/lipics.esa.2024.44","issn":["18688969"],"journal":"Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics","pages":"1-12","publisher":"Schloss Dagstuhl - Leibniz-Zentrum fuer Informatik GmbH","status":"p","title":"Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics","title_org":"Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics (LIPIcs)","volume":"308"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85205695757"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-4448e628-9e81-4a2d-8f2e-7f20d7123fc1"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"An Optimal Randomized Algorithm for Finding the Saddlepoint.","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-818237","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"A saddlepoint of an n × n matrix A is an entry of A that is a maximum in its row and a minimum in its column. Knuth (1968) gave several different algorithms for finding a saddlepoint. The worst-case running time of these algorithms is Θ(n2), and Llewellyn, Tovey, and Trick (1988) showed that this cannot be improved, as in the worst case all entries of A may need to be queried. A strict saddlepoint of A is an entry that is the strict maximum in its row and the strict minimum in its column. The strict saddlepoint (if it exists) is unique, and Bienstock, Chung, Fredman, Schaffer, Shor, and Suri (1991) showed that it can be found in time O(n lg n), where a dominant runtime contribution is sorting the diagonal of the matrix. This upper bound has not been improved since 1991. In this paper we show that the strict saddlepoint can be found in O(n lg* n) ⊂ o(n lg n) time, where lg* denotes the very slowly growing iterated logarithm function, coming close to the lower bound of Ω(n). In fact, we can also compute, within the same runtime, the value of a non-strict saddlepoint, assuming one exists. Our algorithm is based on a simple recursive approach, a feasibility test inspired by searching in sorted matrices, and a relaxed notion of saddlepoint.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Jacob, Riko","Dallant, Justin","Haagensen, Frederik","Kozma, László","Wild, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-759429"],"filters":{"added":"2024-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Belgium","Germany","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Free University of Berlin","University Libre du Bruxelles","University of Liverpool"],"keyword":["Algorithm","MATRIX","saddlepoint"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"finding the saddlepoint faster than sorting","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611977936.17","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-759429","identifier":[{"id":"85183289736","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-f66f2f78-0ae0-4f6f-ac54-202e37adb329"],"keyword":["algorithm","iterated logarithm","matrix","saddlepoint","strict saddlepoint"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-FAD5F7EE6EC9A14292E87908E4DEEAAB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611977936.17","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"be","key":"university libre du bruxelles","loc":["Belgium","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University Libre du Bruxelles","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"free university of berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Free University of Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"gb","key":"university of liverpool","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Liverpool","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Riko","key":"jacobpau","last":"Jacob","locid":"e6f70fb0-caf1-4736-b583-b0777bc1390f","name":"Jacob, Riko","orcid":"0000-0001-9470-1809","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Justin","key":"dallantpau","last":"Dallant","name":"Dallant, Justin","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Frederik","key":"haagensenpau","last":"Haagensen","locid":"672280b6-17c9-4191-9b00-8f1322fc1d90","name":"Haagensen, Frederik","orcid":"0000-0002-4161-4442","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"László","key":"kozmapau","last":"Kozma","name":"Kozma, László","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"wildpau","last":"Wild","name":"Wild, Sebastian","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1137/1.9781611977936.17","eisbn":["9781611977936"],"pages":"168-178","publisher":"Society for Industrial and Applied Mathematics","status":"p","title":"2024 Symposium on Simplicity in Algorithms (SOSA)"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f66f2f78-0ae0-4f6f-ac54-202e37adb329"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Finding the saddlepoint faster than sorting","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-759429","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"We present comparison-based parallel algorithms for sorting n comparable items subject to comparison errors. We consider errors to occur according to a well-studied framework, where the comparison of two elements returns the wrong answer with a fixed probability. In the persistent model, the result of the comparison of two given elements, x and y, always has the same result, and is independent of all other pairs of elements. In the non-persistent model, the result of the comparison of each pair of elements, x and y, is independent of all prior comparisons, including for x and y. It is not possible to always correctly sort a given input set in the persistent model, so we study algorithms that achieve a small maximum dislocation and small total dislocation of the elements in the output permutation. In this paper, we provide parallel algorithms for sorting with comparison errors in the persistent and non-persistent models. Our algorithms are asymptotically optimal in terms of their span, work, and, in the case of persistent errors, maximum and total dislocation. The main results are algorithms for the binary-forking parallel model with atomics, but we also provide algorithms for the CREW PRAM model. Our algorithms include a number of novel techniques and analysis tools, including a PRAM-to-binary-forking-model simulation result, and are the first optimal parallel algorithms for the persistent model and the non-persistent model in the binary-forking parallel model with atomics. In particular, our algorithms have O(log n) span, O(n log n) work, and, in the case of the persistent model, O(log n) maximum dislocation and O(n) total dislocation, with high probability. We achieve similar results for the CREW PRAM model, which are the first optimal methods for the persistent model and the first optimal results for the non-persistent model with reasonable constant factors in the performance bounds.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Jacob, Riko","Goodrich, Michael T."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-712555"],"event":{"acronym":"SPAA","title":"ACM Symposium on Parallelism in Algorithms and Architectures"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of California"],"keyword":["Randomized algorithms","Sorting","algorithm analysis","noisy searching"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"optimal parallel sorting with comparison errors","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3558481.3591093","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-712555","identifier":[{"id":"85164280226","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-cc6e37cb-c41f-4dff-beba-b298cac82e80"],"keyword":["algorithm analysis","noisy searching","randomized algorithms","sorting"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E7708867F03A744ED5B5826151F803F6"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3558481.3591093","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3558481.3591093","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"university of california","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of California","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Riko","key":"jacobpau","last":"Jacob","locid":"e6f70fb0-caf1-4736-b583-b0777bc1390f","name":"Jacob, Riko","orcid":"0000-0001-9470-1809","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael T.","key":"goodrichpau","last":"Goodrich","name":"Goodrich, Michael T.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3558481.3591093","pages":"355-365","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings SPAA '23"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85164280226"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-cc6e37cb-c41f-4dff-beba-b298cac82e80"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Optimal Parallel Sorting with Comparison Errors","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-712555","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"We resolve two open problems in sorting with priced information, introduced by [Charikar, Fagin, Guruswami, Kleinberg, Raghavan, Sahai (CFGKRS), STOC 2000]. In this setting, different comparisons have different (potentially infinite) costs. The goal is to sort with small competitive ratio (algorithmic cost divided by cheapest proof). 1) When all costs are in $\\{0,1,n,\\infty\\}$, we give an algorithm that has $\\widetilde{O}(n^{3/4})$ competitive ratio. Our algorithm generalizes the algorithms for generalized sorting (all costs are either $1$ or $\\infty$), a version initiated by [Huang, Kannan, Khanna, FOCS 2011] and addressed recently by [Kuszmaul, Narayanan, FOCS 2021]. 2) We answer the problem of bichromatic sorting posed by [CFGKRS]: The input is split into $A$ and $B$, and $A-A$ and $B-B$ comparisons are more expensive than an $A-B$ comparisons. We give a randomized algorithm with a O(polylog n) competitive ratio. These results are obtained by introducing the universal sorting problem, which generalizes the existing framework in two important ways. We remove the promise of a directed Hamiltonian path in the DAG of all comparisons. Instead, we require that an algorithm outputs the transitive reduction of the DAG. For bichromatic sorting, when $A-A$ and $B-B$ comparisons cost $\\infty$, this generalizes the well-known nuts and bolts problem. We initiate an instance-based study of the universal sorting problem. Our definition of instance-optimality is inherently more algorithmic than that of the competitive ratio in that we compare the cost of a candidate algorithm to the cost of the optimal instance-aware algorithm. This unifies existing lower bounds, and opens up the possibility of an $O(1)$-instance optimal algorithm for the bichromatic version.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Jacob, Riko","Goswami, Mayank"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381882"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","City University of New York"],"keyword":["Comparison based algorithms","Data Structures and Algorithms","Sorting"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"universal sorting finding a dag using priced comparisons","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2211.04601","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381882","ids":["itu-28e3d92b-0fb2-4001-a253-653ea80fc6a7"],"keyword":["Comparison based algorithms","Data Structures and Algorithms","Sorting"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-39359D7902FA22FF0B9C72236FDDE779"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2211.04601","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"city university of new york","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"City University of New York","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Riko","key":"jacobpau","last":"Jacob","locid":"e6f70fb0-caf1-4736-b583-b0777bc1390f","name":"Jacob, Riko","orcid":"0000-0001-9470-1809","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mayank","key":"goswamipau","last":"Goswami","name":"Goswami, Mayank","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2211.04601","pages":"40","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-28e3d92b-0fb2-4001-a253-653ea80fc6a7"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Universal Sorting: Finding a DAG using Priced Comparisons","type":"Report","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381882","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"The fragile complexity of a comparison-based algorithm is $f(n)$ if each input element participates in $O(f(n))$ comparisons. In this paper, we explore the fragile complexity of algorithms adaptive to various restrictions on the input, i.e., algorithms with a fragile complexity parameterized by a quantity other than the input size~$n$. We show that searching for the predecessor in a sorted array has fragile complexity $\\Theta(\\log k)$, where $k$ is the rank of the query element, both in a randomized and a deterministic setting. For predecessor searches, we also show how to optimally reduce the amortized fragile complexity of the elements in the array. We also prove the following results: Selecting the $k$th smallest element has expected fragile complexity $O(\\log\\log k)$ for the element selected. Deterministically finding the minimum element has fragile complexity $\\Theta(\\log(\\INV))$ and $\\Theta(\\log(\\RUNS))$, where $\\INV$ is the number of inversions in a sequence and $\\RUNS$ is the number of increasing runs in a sequence. Deterministically finding the median has fragile complexity $O(\\log(\\RUNS) + \\log\\log n)$ and $\\Theta(\\log (\\INV))$. Deterministic sorting has fragile complexity $\\Theta(\\log (\\INV))$ but it has fragile complexity $\\Theta(\\log n)$ regardless of the number of runs.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Bose, Prosenjit","Cano, Pilar","Fagerberg, Rolf","Iacono, John","Jacob, Riko","Langerman, Stefan"],"cuid":["S02-099.55-038277"],"filters":{"added":"2022-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Cano, Pilar"],"country":["Belgium","Canada","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Carleton University","New York University","University Libre du Bruxelles","Université Libre de Bruxelles"],"keyword":["Algorithms","Comparison based algorithms","Fragile complexity"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"fragile complexity of adaptive algorithms","src":["itu","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/91125160/2102.00338_1_.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This material is based upon work performed while attending AlgoPARC Workshop on Parallel Algorithms and Data Structures at the University of Hawaii at Manoa, in part supported by the National Science Foundation under Grant No. CCF-1930579 . We thank Timothy Chan and Qizheng He for their ideas for improving the randomized selection algorithm. P.B. was partially supported by NSERC . P.C. and J.I. were supported by F.R.S.- FNRS under Grant No. MISU F 6001 1 . R.F. was partially supported by the Independent Research Fund Denmark , Natural Sciences, grant DFF-7014-00041 . J.I. was supported by NSF grant CCF-1533564 . S.L. is Directeur de Recherches du F.R.S.-FNRS. This material is based upon work performed while attending AlgoPARC Workshop on Parallel Algorithms and Data Structures at the University of Hawaii at Manoa, in part supported by the National Science Foundation under Grant No. CCF-1930579. We thank Timothy Chan and Qizheng He for their ideas for improving the randomized selection algorithm. P.B. was partially supported by NSERC. P.C. and J.I. were supported by F.R.S.-FNRS under Grant No. MISU F 6001 1. R.F. was partially supported by the Independent Research Fund Denmark, Natural Sciences, grant DFF-7014-00041. J.I. was supported by NSF grant CCF-1533564. S.L. is Directeur de Recherches du F.R.S.-FNRS."],"id":"S02-099.55-038277","identifier":[{"id":"85127946803","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-2882662a-bc42-4374-b671-d809ffa2759e","sdu-2a9c2c81-5b44-46b8-915a-304d3ba7c124"],"keyword":["Algorithms","Comparison based algorithms","Fragile complexity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-6FF2C7AB6C8C2E287F7376A6980B3D2B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2102.00338","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/91125160/2102.00338_1_.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ca","key":"carleton university","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Carleton University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"be","key":"université libre de bruxelles","loc":["Belgium","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Université Libre de Bruxelles","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"be","key":"university libre du bruxelles","loc":["Belgium","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University Libre du Bruxelles","used":1},{"affno":"5","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science","SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, Algorithms","SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"us","key":"new york university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"New York University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Riko","key":"jacobpau","last":"Jacob","locid":"e6f70fb0-caf1-4736-b583-b0777bc1390f","name":"Jacob, Riko","orcid":"0000-0001-9470-1809","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Prosenjit","key":"bosepau","last":"Bose","name":"Bose, Prosenjit","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Pilar","key":"canopau","last":"Cano","name":"Cano, Pilar","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rolf","key":"fagerbergpau","last":"Fagerberg","locid":"19291dfb-bacd-4983-a78c-8c80787b3c8c","name":"Fagerberg, Rolf","orcid":"0000-0003-1004-3314","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 6 4","corresponding":null,"first":"John","key":"iaconopau","last":"Iacono","name":"Iacono, John","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefan","key":"langermanpau","last":"Langerman","name":"Langerman, Stefan","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.tcs.2022.03.034","issn":["03043975"],"journal":"Theoretical Computer Science","pages":"92-102","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Theoretical Computer Science","volume":"915"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85127946803"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-2882662a-bc42-4374-b671-d809ffa2759e","sdu-2a9c2c81-5b44-46b8-915a-304d3ba7c124"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Fragile complexity of adaptive algorithms","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.55-038277","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"We prove an Ω (log n log log n) lower bound for the span of implementing the n input, log n-depth FFT circuit (also known as butterfly network) in the nonatomic binary fork-join model. In this model, memory-access synchronizations occur only through fork operations, which spawn two child threads, and join operations, which resume a parent thread when its child threads terminate. Our bound is asymptotically tight for the nonatomic binary fork-join model, which has been of interest of late, due to its conceptual elegance and ability to capture asynchrony. Our bound implies super-logarithmic lower bound in the nonatomic binary fork-join model for implementing the butterfly merging networks used, e.g., in Batcher's bitonic and odd-even mergesort networks. This lower bound also implies an asymptotic separation result for the atomic and nonatomic versions of the fork-join model, since, as we point out, FFT circuits can be implemented in the atomic binary fork-join model with span equal to their circuit depth.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Jacob, Riko","Sitchinava, Nodari","Goodrich, Michael T."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383824"],"event":{"acronym":"SODA","title":"ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of California","University of Hawaii"],"keyword":["lower bound parallel computation fork join model"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"atomic power in forks a super logarithmic lower bound for implementing butterfly networks in the nonatomic binary fork join model","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611976465.128","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383824","identifier":[{"id":"85105275419","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-af9ea194-c21a-407c-bb5d-8a636b19044c"],"keyword":["lower bound, parallel computation, fork-join model"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-CCDA83AB7120E1E15B24CD0820B0FCE4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86579735/p21_soda.pdf","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611976465.128","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"university of hawaii","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Hawaii","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"university of california","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of California","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Riko","key":"jacobpau","last":"Jacob","locid":"e6f70fb0-caf1-4736-b583-b0777bc1390f","name":"Jacob, Riko","orcid":"0000-0001-9470-1809","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nodari","key":"sitchinavapau","last":"Sitchinava","name":"Sitchinava, Nodari","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael T.","key":"goodrichpau","last":"Goodrich","name":"Goodrich, Michael T.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1137/1.9781611976465.128","pages":"2141-2153","publisher":"Society for Industrial and Applied Mathematics","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2021 ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms (SODA)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-af9ea194-c21a-407c-bb5d-8a636b19044c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Atomic Power in Forks: A Super-Logarithmic Lower Bound for Implementing Butterfly Networks in the Nonatomic Binary Fork-Join Model","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383824","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"The fragile complexity of a comparison-based algorithm is f(n) if each input element participates in O(f(n)) comparisons. In this paper, we explore the fragile complexity of algorithms adaptive to various restrictions on the input, i.e., algorithms with a fragile complexity parameterized by a quantity other than the input size n. We show that searching for the predecessor in a sorted array has fragile complexity Θ(logk), where k is the rank of the query element, both in a randomized and a deterministic setting. For predecessor searches, we also show how to optimally reduce the amortized fragile complexity of the elements in the array. We also prove the following results: Selecting the kth smallest element has expected fragile complexity O(loglogk) for the element selected. Deterministically finding the minimum element has fragile complexity Θ(log(Inv)) and Θ(log(Runs)), where Inv is the number of inversions in a sequence and Runs is the number of increasing runs in a sequence. Deterministically finding the median has fragile complexity O(log(Runs)+loglogn) and Θ(log(Inv)). Deterministic sorting has fragile complexity Θ(log(Inv)) but it has fragile complexity Θ(logn) regardless of the number of runs.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Jacob, Riko","Fagerberg, Rolf","Bose, Prosenjit","Cano, Pilar","Iacono, John","Langerman, Stefan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383842"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Belgium","Canada","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Carleton University","New York University","University Libre du Bruxelles"],"keyword":["Algorithms","Comparison based algorithms","Fragile complexity"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"fragile complexity of adaptive algorithms","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/348959027_Fragile_Complexity_of_Adaptive_Algorithms","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383842","identifier":[{"id":"85106173925","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-b096b2bc-e2f6-4fc3-b7f6-460c13d33ae1"],"keyword":["Algorithms","Comparison based algorithms","Fragile complexity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-5-60BC37B833F704016A1204904C6C7131"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/348959027_Fragile_Complexity_of_Adaptive_Algorithms","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86579703/2102.00338.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["University of Southern Denmark"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"ca","key":"carleton university","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Carleton University","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"be","key":"university libre du bruxelles","loc":["Belgium","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University Libre du Bruxelles","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"us","key":"new york university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"New York University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Riko","key":"jacobpau","last":"Jacob","locid":"e6f70fb0-caf1-4736-b583-b0777bc1390f","name":"Jacob, Riko","orcid":"0000-0001-9470-1809","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Rolf","key":"fagerbergpau","last":"Fagerberg","name":"Fagerberg, Rolf","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Prosenjit","key":"bosepau","last":"Bose","name":"Bose, Prosenjit","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Pilar","key":"canopau","last":"Cano","name":"Cano, Pilar","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4 5","corresponding":null,"first":"John","key":"iaconopau","last":"Iacono","name":"Iacono, John","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefan","key":"langermanpau","last":"Langerman","name":"Langerman, Stefan","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9783030752422"],"isbn":["9783030752415"],"pages":"144-157","publisher":"Springer","series":"LNCS","status":"p","title":"International Conference on Algorithms and Complexity","volume":"LNCS 12701"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85106173925"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b096b2bc-e2f6-4fc3-b7f6-460c13d33ae1"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Fragile Complexity of Adaptive Algorithms","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383842","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"The fragile complexity of a comparison-based algorithm is f(n) if each input element participates in O(f(n)) comparisons. In this paper, we explore the fragile complexity of algorithms adaptive to various restrictions on the input, i.e., algorithms with a fragile complexity parameterized by a quantity other than the input size n. We show that searching for the predecessor in a sorted array has fragile complexity Θ(log k), where k is the rank of the query element, both in a randomized and a deterministic setting. For predecessor searches, we also show how to optimally reduce the amortized fragile complexity of the elements in the array. We also prove the following results: Selecting the kth smallest element has expected fragile complexity O(log log k) for the element selected. Deterministically finding the minimum element has fragile complexity Θ(log (Inv ) ) and Θ(log (Runs ) ), where Inv is the number of inversions in a sequence and Runs is the number of increasing runs in a sequence. Deterministically finding the median has fragile complexity O(log (Runs ) + log log n) and Θ(log (Inv ) ). Deterministic sorting has fragile complexity Θ(log (Inv ) ) but it has fragile complexity Θ(log n) regardless of the number of runs.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Bose, Prosenjit","Cano, Pilar","Fagerberg, Rolf","Iacono, John","Jacob, Riko","Langerman, Stefan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-581773"],"event":{"acronym":"CIAC","end":"2021-05-12","place":"Virtual, Online","start":"2021-05-10","title":"12th International Conference on Algorithms and Complexity, CIAC 2021","year":"2021"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Cano, Pilar"],"country":["Belgium","Canada","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Carleton University","New York University","Université Libre de Bruxelles"],"keyword":["Algorithms","Comparison based algorithms","Fragile complexity"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"fragile complexity of adaptive algorithms","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/226844951/978_3_030_75242_2.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This material is based upon work performed while attending AlgoPARC Workshop on Parallel Algorithms and Data Structures at the University of Hawaii at Manoa, in part supported by the National Science Foundation under Grant No. CCF-1930579. We thank Timothy Chan and Qizheng He for their ideas improving the randomized selection algorithm. P.B was partially supported by NSERC. P.C and J.I. were supported by F.R.S.-FNRS under Grant no MISU F 6001 1. R.F. was partially supported by the Independent Research Fund Denmark, Natural Sciences, grant DFF-7014-00041. J.I. was supported by NSF grant CCF-1533564. S.L. is Directeur de Recherches du F.R.S.-FNRS."],"id":"S01-000.00-581773","identifier":[{"id":"85106173925","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-1de60fdb-542e-470d-bd75-4959dd3bb157"],"keyword":["Algorithms","Comparison based algorithms","Fragile complexity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-5-60BC37B833F704016A1204904C6C7131"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/226844951/978_3_030_75242_2.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2102.00338","version":"Pre-print"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"ca","key":"carleton university","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Carleton University","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"be","key":"université libre de bruxelles","loc":["Belgium","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Université Libre de Bruxelles","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science","SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"new york university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"New York University","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Prosenjit","key":"bosepau","last":"Bose","name":"Bose, Prosenjit","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Pilar","key":"canopau","last":"Cano","name":"Cano, Pilar","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rolf","key":"fagerbergpau","last":"Fagerberg","locid":"19291dfb-bacd-4983-a78c-8c80787b3c8c","name":"Fagerberg, Rolf","orcid":"0000-0003-1004-3314","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 4","corresponding":null,"first":"John","key":"iaconopau","last":"Iacono","name":"Iacono, John","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"first":"Riko","key":"jacobpau","last":"Jacob","name":"Jacob, Riko","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefan","key":"langermanpau","last":"Langerman","name":"Langerman, Stefan","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Tiziana","key":"calamoneripedh","last":"Calamoneri","name":"Calamoneri, Tiziana","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Federico","key":"coròpedh","last":"Corò","name":"Corò, Federico","rank":8,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-75242-2_10","eisbn":["9783030752422"],"isbn":["9783030752415"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"144-157","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"Algorithms and Complexity - 12th International Conference, CIAC 2021, Proceedings"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85106173925"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-1de60fdb-542e-470d-bd75-4959dd3bb157"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Fragile Complexity of Adaptive Algorithms","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-581773","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"We consider static, external memory indexes for exact and approximate versions of the k-nearest neighbor (k-NN) problem, and show new lower bounds under a standard indivisibility assumption: Polynomial space indexing schemes for high-dimensional k-NN in Hamming space cannot take advantage of block transfers: í(k) block reads are needed to to answer a query. For the l∞ metric the lower bound holds even if we allow c-appoximate nearest neighbors to be returned, for c ∈ (1, 3). The restriction to c < 3 is necessary: For every metric there exists an indexing scheme in the indexability model of Hellerstein et al. using space O(kn), where n is the number of points, that can retrieve k 3-approximate nearest neighbors using optimal ⌈k/B⌉ I/Os, where B is the block size. For specific metrics, data structures with better approximation factors are possible. For k-NN in Hamming space and every approximation factor c>1 there exists a polynomial space data structure that returns k c-approximate nearest neighbors in ⌈k/B⌉ I/Os. To show these lower bounds we develop two new techniques: First, to handle that approximation algorithms have more freedom in deciding which result set to return we develop a relaxed version of the λ-set workload technique of Hellerstein et al. This technique allows us to show lower bounds that hold in d ≥ n dimensions. To extend the lower bounds down to d = O(k log(n/k)) dimensions, we develop a new deterministic dimension reduction technique that may be of independent interest.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Goswami, Mayank","Jacob, Riko","Pagh, Rasmus"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381846"],"event":{"acronym":"PODS","title":"ACM SIGMOD-SIGACT-SIGAI Symposium on Principles of Database Systems"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","City University of New York"],"keyword":["Dimension reduction","Hamming space","L metric","approximate nearest neighbors","block reads","external memory indexes","indexing schemes","indivisibility assumption","k nearest neighbor k NN","polynomial space data structures"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on the i o complexity of the k nearest neighbors problem","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85551197/2002.04870.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381846","identifier":[{"id":"85086280099","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-261aa4d2-051e-49bf-b9ab-336e3b73ff14"],"keyword":["Hamming space","approximate nearest neighbors","block reads","dimension reduction","external memory indexes","indexing schemes","indivisibility assumption","k-nearest neighbor (k-NN)","l∞ metric","polynomial space data structures"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-73C02757EE4E33CDAA1592F0E9E5BE2E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85551197/2002.04870.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"city university of new york","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"City University of New York","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mayank","key":"goswamipau","last":"Goswami","name":"Goswami, Mayank","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Riko","key":"jacobpau","last":"Jacob","locid":"e6f70fb0-caf1-4736-b583-b0777bc1390f","name":"Jacob, Riko","orcid":"0000-0001-9470-1809","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus","key":"paghpau","last":"Pagh","name":"Pagh, Rasmus","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3375395.3387649","eisbn":["9781450371087"],"pages":"205-212","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","series":"ACM SIGMOD-SIGACT-SIGAI Symposium on Principles of Database Systems","status":"p","title":"PODS'20: Proceedings of the 39th ACM SIGMOD-SIGACT-SIGAI Symposium on Principles of Database Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85086280099"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-261aa4d2-051e-49bf-b9ab-336e3b73ff14"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"On the I/O Complexity of the k-Nearest Neighbors Problem","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381846","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"We initiate a study of algorithms with a focus on the computational complexity of individual elements, and introduce the fragile complexity of comparison-based algorithms as the maximal number of comparisons any individual element takes part in. We give a number of upper and lower bounds on the fragile complexity for fundamental problems, including Minimum, Selection, Sorting and Heap Construction. The results include both deterministic and randomized upper and lower bounds, and demonstrate a separation between the two settings for a number of problems. The depth of a comparator network is a straight-forward upper bound on the worst case fragile complexity of the corresponding fragile algorithm. We prove that fragile complexity is a different and strictly easier property than the depth of comparator networks, in the sense that for some problems a fragile complexity equal to the best network depth can be achieved with less total work and that with randomization, even a lower fragile complexity is possible.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Afshani, Peyman","Fagerberg, Rolf","Hammer, David","Jacob, Riko","Kostitsyna, Irina","Meyer, Ulrich","Penschuck, Manuel","Sitchinava, Nodari"],"cuid":["S03-095.83-030108"],"event":{"acronym":"ESA","end":"2019-09-11","place":"Munich/Garching, de","start":"2019-09-09","title":"27th Annual European Symposium on Algorithms, ESA 2019","year":"2019"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Germany","Netherlands","Sweden","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Eindhoven University of Technology","Goethe University Frankfurt","University of Gothenburg","University of Hawaii","University of Hawaii at Manoa"],"keyword":["Algorithms","Comparator Networks","Comparison based algorithms","Computational complexity","Deterministic and Randomized Bounds","Fragile complexity","Lower bounds"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"fragile complexity of comparison based algorithms","src":["au","itu","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ESA.2019.2","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["∗ This material is based upon work partially performed while attending AlgoPARC Workshop on Parallel Algorithms and Data Structures at the University of Hawaii at Manoa, in part supported by the National Science Foundation under Grant No. CCF-1745331. Funding Rolf Fagerberg: Supported by the Independent Research Fund Denmark, Natural Sciences, grant DFF-7014-00041. David Hammer: Supported by the Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft (DFG) under grants ME 2088/3-2 and ME 2088/4-2. Ulrich Meyer: Supported by the Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft (DFG) under grants ME 2088/3-2 and ME 2088/4-2. Manuel Penschuck: Supported by the Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft (DFG) under grants ME 2088/3-2 and ME 2088/4-2. Nodari Sitchinava: Supported by the National Science Foundation under Grant No. CCF-1533823."],"id":"S03-095.83-030108","identifier":[{"id":"85074867612","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-f0ddc06c-5660-446d-847b-a1072d9cc0cd","itu-dce87c22-5b0e-4126-8548-dde5f57b98c7","sdu-1cab1b10-ec5f-4b28-80eb-0046fb04fb79"],"keyword":["Algorithms","Comparator Networks","Comparison based algorithms","Computational Complexity","Deterministic and Randomized Bounds","Fragile Complexity","Lower bounds"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-ED4B4010E6E0FC89FDBB6692B655E9E1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findr